Two comments here:

First, we are *not* dealing with censorship or violation of anyone's
rights, as far as I can see. If a publication is going out with the MIT
name on it, and is produced with the support of MIT money, it is MIT's
right to veto it. This isn't to say that the decision is reasonable, only
that MIT is within its rights.

It's reminiscent of another Freshman Picture Book incident which took
place in (I think) the early 80's. The FPB traditionally runs a few joke
pictures (or at least was doing so at that time), and included a picture
of a gorilla captioned "Harvey Grogo, Kampala, Uganda." Grogo the gorilla
was the mascot of Technique, the MIT yearbook. Some black students saw
this as an insult and demanded the expulsion of all TCA members from MIT
(I am not making this up). The MIT faculty humiliated the editor of the
FPB and required him to make a public apology. 

One professor wrote a letter to The Tech explaining the "reasoning" by
which he concluded that the picture was racist. It went roughly as
follows: The picture shows a gorilla from Uganda. We may conclude from it
that residents of Uganda are gorillas. Since the population of Uganda
includes black people, black people are gorillas. Since black people are
people, people are gorillas.

The same FPB also showed a blank picture, allegedly representing the
invisible Herman Q. Witherspoon, from Alaska. Someone pointed out that the
same logic could be applied here: Since the non-existent person here is
portrayed as being from Alaska, the picture is saying that Alaskans do not
exist. Since Alaskans are people, people do not exist.

(In both cases, I'm condensing the chain of fallacies, which was expounded
at ludicrous length in the professor's original letter.)

In the Grogo case, I think the student should have told MIT that if they
expelled him, he would sue for breach of contract. (No threat to expel or
suspend students was mentioned in the current case.) But again, it's not
censorship.

-- 
          Gary McGath
          gmcgath@condes.mv.com

