Actually having read this story, I think that MIT handled this
whole situation rather well.  I think the cover would surely have 
been interpreted as offensive, even by those not so sensitive 
about such issues- I would have been offended.  It would have been
a whole lot better, though, if the administration had known about
the cover illustration ahead of time and then more options could
have been given- plenty of time for the editors to learn what
'intuitively obvious' means to minority students and then have time
to redo the cover to everyone's satisfaction.  The president 
compromised with the students by offering to defray new printing
costs and by saying that he would feel responsible if students
were offended by this blunder- after all the students who were editing
the publication didn't sound to me as if they were about to take 
responsibility for what they printed.

Yes, it is censorship,
technically, but really rather a mild example of it; not something
worthy of the student body taking over the president's office or
marching on Washington (it's a little different from a senseless
war or the AIDS crisis).

